{"qid":"Q148540","names":["Republic of Florence"],"title":"Republic of Florence","endonym":{"native":"Repubblica fiorentina","lang":"it"},"capsule":"The Republic of Florence (Repubblica fiorentina) was a communal republic in Tuscany that emerged from the collapse of Matildine authority around 1115, developing into one of medieval Europe's most powerful banking and textile centers. Under the Medici family — who dominated politics without formal title from 1434 under Cosimo de' Medici and then as dukes from 1531 — Florence oscillated between republican and signorial government; the republic was formally re-established in 1494 after Charles VIII's invasion expelled Piero de' Medici, and again briefly in 1527–1530 before the final Medici restoration under Alessandro de' Medici with Spanish support. The republic was extinguished when Alessandro was proclaimed hereditary Duke of Florence in 1532, and its dissolution was completed when Cosimo I established the Grand Duchy of Tuscany in 1569. Florence's republican period produced Dante, Petrarch, Machiavelli, and Brunelleschi.","attestation":"Boundaries from Seshat Cliopatria (Bennett et al., Scientific Data 2025; CC BY 4.0), digitized from standard historical atlases at ~0.07° resolution. The territorial extent of the Republic of Florence is well documented from Florentine cadastral records, communal statutes, and extensive diplomatic correspondence, though its boundaries expanded significantly through conquest over its six centuries.","attestationLinks":[{"label":"Cliopatria dataset (Seshat, CC BY 4.0)","url":"https://github.com/Seshat-Global-History-Databank/cliopatria"},{"label":"Bennett et al. 2025, Scientific Data","url":"https://www.nature.com/articles/s41597-025-04516-9"}],"citations":[{"label":"Wikipedia: Republic of Florence","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Republic_of_Florence"},{"label":"Wikipedia: Medici","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Medici"},{"label":"Wikipedia: Grand Duchy of Tuscany","url":"http